<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toungoo dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1510">1510</a>–<a href="/years/1752">1752</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Taungoo dynasty, Nyaungyan dynasty, Second Burmese Empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Toungoo dynasty was the ruling dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> (Myanmar) from the mid-16th century to 1752, in two periods: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> (1510–1599) and the Nyaungyan Restoration (1599–1752). King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mingyi-nyo">Mingyi Nyo</a> founded it at Taungoo, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> reunified the territories of the Pagan Kingdom and brought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan States</a>, Manipur, Siam and Lan Xang. The empire collapsed after Bayinnaung's death in 1581, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyaungyan-min">Nyaungyan Min</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaukpetlun">Anaukpetlun</a> restored a smaller kingdom based in Ava. After a Mon rebellion founded the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom in 1740, its armies captured Inwa in 1752 and ended the dynasty.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1548 Tabinshwehti of the <b>Toungoo dynasty</b> besieged the capital, a campaign remembered for the death of Queen Suriyothai in combat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
